    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d250">

                <pb facs="0459" n="435" sameAs="frus:frus1950v03/pg435"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.5/11–850: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The United States Deputy Representative on the North
                        Atlantic Council</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Spofford</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1950-11-08T18:00:00+00:00">November 8, 1950—6
                            p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p> Depto 155. Hoyer-Millar<anchor n="1" xml:id="d250-f1"/> and Rose<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d250-f2"/> of Foreign Office state that Bevin has not yet reached
                    decision on “where we go next” after Defense <pb facs="0460" n="436"
                        xml:id="pg436"/> Committee meeting pending full discussion with Shinwell who
                    returned only yesterday. Foreign Office however thinking tentatively along
                    following lines: <list>
                        <label>1.</label>
                        <item>Earliest conclusion of Schuman Plan most desirable but British fear
                            any pressure from them to this end might not be well received. They
                            would welcome US action.</item>
                        <label>2.</label>
                        <item>French must obviously be persuaded to negotiate, and only sound basis
                            for negotiation would be American proposals, incorporating additional
                            safeguards and taking account of both French and German views.</item>
                        <label>3.</label>
                        <item>Ways must be found of saving French Government’s face. Pleven Plan was
                            obviously completely impracticable but could not be publicly rejected
                            without freezing this and future French Governments in opposition. If
                            any parts of it could be soundly adapted and incorporated firmly in NATO
                            framework, so much the better. If not, perhaps parts could be accepted
                            in principle as goals to be worked toward rather than as conditions
                            precedent to prompt action.</item>
                        <label>4.</label>
                        <item>British had gained impression in Washington we considered early
                            solution German participation issue necessary in connection with our
                            legislative program and would welcome further information as to our
                            thinking on timing and legislative schedule.</item>
                        <label>5.</label>
                        <item>They stated Bevin had made no visible impression or progress with Guy
                                Mollet.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d250-f3"/>
                        </item>
                    </list>
                </p>
                <p>Sent Department Depto 155, repeated info Paris 833, Frankfort 350.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>[<hi rendition="#small-caps">Spofford</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d250-f1" n="1" xml:id="d250-f1-content">
                <p>Derick Hoyer Millar, U.K. Deputy Representative on the North Atlantic
                    Council.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d250-f2" n="2" xml:id="d250-f2-content">
                <p>Edward M. Rose, of the Western Union Secretariat of the British Foreign
                    Office.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d250-f3" n="3" xml:id="d250-f3-content">
                <p>Secretary General of the Socialist Party in France, appointed Minister of State
                    for European Affairs in Pleven Cabinet, July 12, 1950.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
